---
title: Timeseries Engine
description: Peer engine for time-ordered data with retention, continuous aggregation, ILP ingest, and PromQL.
---

# Timeseries Engine

Timeseries is a peer engine sharing storage with `columnar` and `spatial`. It adds retention policies, continuous aggregation, ILP ingest, and dedicated time-series SQL functions. Data is stored in the same compressed-column memtables with a `TIME_KEY` column driving partition-by-time and block-level skip.

Selected via `WITH (engine='timeseries')` on `CREATE COLLECTION`, or the `CREATE TIMESERIES <name>` convenience alias.

## DDL

```sql
CREATE COLLECTION cpu_metrics (
    ts TIMESTAMP TIME_KEY,
    host VARCHAR,
    region VARCHAR,
    cpu_usage FLOAT,
    mem_usage FLOAT
) WITH (engine='timeseries', partition_by='1d', retention='90d');

-- Convenience alias
CREATE TIMESERIES cpu_metrics;
```

## Partitions

Rows land in an in-memory buffer first. A flush of that buffer writes one sealed partition for each `partition_by` interval its rows fall in. The partitions of one flush appear together: a crash leaves all of them or none.

| `partition_by`       | Interval                                                        |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| not set              | One hour. A flush whose rows span several hours writes one partition per hour. |
| `30s`, `15m`, `1h`, `1d`, `1w` | A fixed duration, aligned to multiples of itself from the Unix epoch. |
| `1M`                 | A calendar month, starting at 00:00 UTC on the first day.      |
| `1y`                 | A calendar year, starting at 00:00 UTC on 1 January.          |
| `UNBOUNDED`          | One partition per flush.                                        |
| `AUTO`               | Starts at one day. It narrows to one hour once a core ingests more than 100,000 rows a second. |

- `ALTER TIMESERIES <name> SET (partition_by = '…')` applies to later flushes only. A partition already on disk keeps the interval it was cut at.
- A late row, one whose interval an earlier flush already sealed, forms a new partition in that interval. A sealed partition is never rewritten to take it.

```sql
-- List the partitions of a timeseries collection
SHOW PARTITIONS FOR cpu_metrics;

-- Rewrite every sealed partition with the current schema and codecs
REWRITE PARTITIONS FOR cpu_metrics;
```

`SHOW PARTITIONS FOR` returns one row per partition:

| Column        | Meaning                                                                 |
| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `partition`   | The partition name.                                                     |
| `state`       | `Active`, `Sealed` or `Merged`.                                         |
| `min_ts`      | The earliest row timestamp, in milliseconds.                            |
| `max_ts`      | The latest row timestamp, in milliseconds.                              |
| `rows`        | The row count.                                                          |
| `size`        | The size on disk.                                                       |
| `interval_ms` | The width of the interval the partition was cut at. `0` means `UNBOUNDED`. |

`REWRITE PARTITIONS FOR` rewrites each sealed partition and drops every column the collection no longer has. The rows do not change. The statement returns after the last partition is rewritten and reports how many partitions it rewrote. It needs the `ALTER` privilege on the collection.

Both statements read the collection in the current database. Neither runs inside a transaction. `SHOW PARTITIONS ON <stream>` is a different statement: it lists a change stream's partitions (see [Consumer Groups](../real-time/consumer-groups)).

## Queries

```sql
-- Time-bucketed aggregation
SELECT time_bucket('5 minutes', ts) AS bucket, host, AVG(cpu_usage) AS avg_cpu
FROM cpu_metrics
WHERE ts > now() - INTERVAL '1 hour'
GROUP BY bucket, host ORDER BY bucket DESC;

-- Approximate aggregation (mergeable across shards)
SELECT approx_count_distinct(host), approx_percentile(cpu_usage, 0.95)
FROM cpu_metrics WHERE ts > now() - INTERVAL '24 hours';
```

## Continuous Aggregation

A continuous aggregate folds new rows into per-bucket state. It never re-scans the source.

```sql
CREATE CONTINUOUS AGGREGATE cpu_hourly ON cpu_metrics
  BUCKET '1h'
  AGGREGATE avg(cpu_usage) AS avg_cpu, ts_percentile(cpu_usage, 0.99) AS p99_cpu
  GROUP BY host
  WITH (refresh_interval = '1m', retention = '365d');

REFRESH CONTINUOUS AGGREGATE cpu_hourly;
```

`BUCKET` sets the bucket width. Do not use `time_bucket(...)` in the definition.

| Clause | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| `AGGREGATE f(col) [AS alias], ...` | Outputs. Functions: `count`, `sum`, `avg`, `min`, `max`, `first`, `last`, `count_distinct`, `approx_count_distinct`, `percentile(col, q)` (also `ts_percentile`, `approx_percentile`), `topk(col, k)` (also `approx_topk`). `q` is in `[0, 1]`. `k` is at least 1. Use `count(*)` to count rows. |
| `GROUP BY col, ...` | Optional. Adds group columns to each bucket. |
| `refresh_policy` | `on_flush` (default), `on_seal`, `manual`, or an interval such as `'5m'` (folds periodically). |
| `refresh_interval` | Interval such as `'1m'`. Folds periodically on the maintenance tick. |
| `retention` | Interval after which the aggregate drops old buckets. |

Refresh behavior:

- `on_flush` and `on_seal` fold each flushed partition once.
- An interval `refresh_policy` or `refresh_interval` folds on the maintenance tick at that interval.
- `manual` folds only on `REFRESH CONTINUOUS AGGREGATE`. A read serves the last refresh.

Aggregate state survives restart. It keeps its history after raw retention removes the source rows.

`SELECT * FROM cpu_hourly` returns one row per bucket and group. Columns are `bucket`, the `GROUP BY` columns, then the outputs. The aggregate is read-only.

## Timeseries SQL Functions

| Function               | What it does                         |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `ts_rate`              | Per-second rate of change            |
| `ts_delta`             | Difference between consecutive values|
| `ts_moving_avg`        | Moving average over a window         |
| `ts_ema`               | Exponential moving average           |
| `ts_interpolate`       | Gap-fill with interpolated values    |
| `ts_percentile`        | Percentile calculation               |
| `ts_zscore`            | Z-score anomaly detection            |
| `ts_bollinger_upper/lower/mid/width` | Bollinger Bands       |
| `ts_moving_percentile` | Rolling percentile                   |
| `ts_correlate`         | Correlation between two series       |
| `ts_lag` / `ts_lead`   | Previous/next value in a series      |

## ILP Ingest

Enable with `ports.ilp = 8086`. Any ILP-compatible client (Telegraf, Vector) pushes metrics directly:

```bash
echo "cpu,host=web-01 usage=72.5 1609459200000000000" | nc localhost 8086
```

Adaptive batching and per-series core routing — self-tuning, no configuration needed.

## Grafana / PromQL

NodeDB works as a native Grafana Prometheus data source at `http://nodedb:6480/v1/obsv/api`. Full PromQL engine (Tier 1+2+3 functions). Also supports Prometheus remote write/read for long-term storage.
